Since {A} measures {B}, let it measure it according to {C}.

I say that {C} is not odd.

If possible, let it be so. Then, since {A} measures {B} according to {C}, therefore {A} multiplied by {C} makes {B}. Therefore {B} is made up of odd numbers the multitude of which is odd. Therefore {B} is odd, which is absurd, for by hypothesis it is even. Therefore {C} is not odd, therefore {C} is even. [!just IX.23]

Thus {A} measures {B} an even number of times. For this reason then it also measures the half of it.

Therefore, *if an odd number measures an even number, then it also measures half of it.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
